<BaseLayout title="How to Build a Client Onboarding Process | AI Workflow Toolkit" description="A practical seven-step client onboarding process for consultants, freelancers and small agencies.">
  <div class="wrap"><article class="article"><div class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Client operations · 6 min read</span><h1>How to build a client onboarding process that <span class="gradient">scales.</span></h1><p>A good onboarding process reduces uncertainty for both sides. It sets expectations early, collects the information you need and gives the project a clear first milestone.</p></div>
  <h2>1. Start with the outcome</h2><p>Before you send forms or schedule meetings, write down the outcome the client is paying for. Agree on what success looks like, what is included and what falls outside the scope. Clear boundaries now prevent awkward surprises later.</p>
  <h2>2. Make one welcome message do useful work</h2><p>Send a concise welcome note with the next steps, main contact, communication channel, expected response times and the date of the kickoff. Link to one shared project space rather than scattering instructions across messages.</p>
  <h2>3. Collect only the information you need</h2><p>Prepare a short intake form for goals, audience, existing assets, relevant accounts and constraints. Avoid asking for passwords in forms or email. Use approved access invitations and a reputable password manager when access is genuinely needed.</p>
  <h2>4. Define roles and approvals</h2><p>Identify the person who can make decisions, who provides feedback and who gives final approval. Set review windows and explain how feedback should be consolidated. This is especially useful when several client stakeholders are involved.</p>
  <h2>5. Put the timeline somewhere visible</h2><p>Break the work into milestones with an owner, target date and dependency. Include the client's responsibilities, such as supplying content or approving a draft. A timeline is a shared agreement, not just an internal reminder.</p>
  <h2>6. Run a focused kickoff</h2><p>Use the kickoff to confirm priorities, open questions, risks, deliverables and the next decision. Share a short written summary afterward so both sides have the same understanding.</p>
  <h2>7. Improve the process after each project</h2><p>Track where projects stall: missing assets, delayed approvals, unclear scope or repeated questions. Change the checklist based on real friction. Keep the process lightweight enough that your team will actually use it.</p>
